<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edict of Nantes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1598</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a> was issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a> in April 1598 to end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a>. It granted the Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> freedom of conscience, rights of worship in defined places, civil equality and fortified towns as security. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> revoked it in 1685 with the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a>, driving many Huguenots abroad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes.jpg" alt="Edict of Nantes" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry IV of France. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edit_de_nantes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
